---
layout: article
title: Alerts and notifications
description: Review Appwrite monitor alerts, notify organization members, and use escalations to reach the right people when an alert keeps firing.
---

An alert records one period during which a monitor's condition is met or an endpoint check is failing. Appwrite opens the alert as **Firing** and marks it **Resolved** when a check passes again. If the monitor fires again after it recovers, Appwrite opens a new alert.

# Review and acknowledge alerts {% #review-and-acknowledge-alerts %}

1. In your project, open **Observe** > **Monitors** and select the **Alerts** tab.
2. Filter by **All**, **Firing**, or **Resolved**, then select an alert to see when it started, the usage reading or check result that opened it, its peak, and how many notifications went out. For a resolved alert, you also see when it recovered.
3. For a firing alert, click **Acknowledge** to show your team that someone is handling it. The alert keeps firing until the monitor recovers. Acknowledging also stops the alert's escalation.

{% only_dark %}
![Alerts tab with a firing executions alert today, a resolved API health check alert from yesterday, and a resolved bandwidth alert, grouped by date](/images/docs/monitors/dark/alert-history.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Alerts tab with a firing executions alert today, a resolved API health check alert from yesterday, and a resolved bandwidth alert, grouped by date](/images/docs/monitors/alert-history.avif)
{% /only_light %}

You can also acknowledge an alert by right-clicking it in the list, or from the **Open alerts** card on the monitor's page. To revert, use **Undo acknowledgement**. Once an alert resolves, its acknowledgement can't change. The count on the **Alerts** tab only includes firing alerts that nobody has acknowledged.

Deleting a monitor resolves its open alert without notifying anyone, and keeps its alert history.

# Choose who gets notified {% #choose-who-gets-notified %}

Choose recipients under **Notify** when you create a monitor. To change them later, open the monitor and go to **Settings** > **Notifications** > **Recipients**. You can pick the **Owners** and **Developers** roles of your organization, or an [escalation](#escalate-an-alert).

Confirmed members in the selected roles get an email and a Console notification once when an alert fires, and again when it resolves. Remove every recipient to keep alerts in the Console and use their [project events](#use-project-events) without notifying anyone directly.

On Appwrite Cloud, members also get a push notification in Appwrite Beep, the Appwrite Cloud app on the [App Store](https://apps.apple.com/app/id6831213827) and [Google Play](https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=io.appwrite.beep). From a push, they can acknowledge, snooze, or mute an alert.

Each monitor has a **Severity** of **Info**, **Warning**, **Error**, or **Critical**. On Android, Beep rings critical alerts through silent mode.

**Cooldown** sets the minimum time between notifications for two alerts from the same monitor. If the monitor fires again within the cooldown, Appwrite records the alert and notifies your team once the cooldown ends, if the alert is still firing. Set the cooldown under **Name, cooldown** when you create a monitor, or in **Settings** > **Notifications** later.

# Escalate an alert {% #escalate-an-alert %}

An escalation notifies people in steps until someone acknowledges the alert. Escalations belong to the organization, so monitors in any of its projects can use them.

1. In your organization, open **Settings** > **Escalations** and click **Create escalation**.
2. Enter a name and add steps. For each step, choose the roles or members to notify and how many seconds to **Wait**. The first step waits from when the alert fires, and each later step from the step before it.
3. Under **Repeat**, choose how many times to run the steps again, up to 9. Each repeat starts from the first step, one monitor cooldown after the last step.
4. Click **Create**.

{% only_dark %}
![Create escalation dialog that notifies developers when an alert fires, then owners and Priya Shah 900 seconds later, and repeats once](/images/docs/monitors/dark/escalation.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Create escalation dialog that notifies developers when an alert fires, then owners and Priya Shah 900 seconds later, and repeats once](/images/docs/monitors/escalation.avif)
{% /only_light %}

To use the escalation, open a monitor and go to **Settings** > **Notifications**. In **Recipients**, click **Add**, select the escalation under **Escalations**, and click **Update**. An escalation replaces the roles the monitor notified before. You can also pick an escalation under **Notify** when you create a monitor.

Appwrite follows the steps while the alert is firing, and stops when someone acknowledges the alert or it resolves. When it resolves, everyone the escalation reached hears about it. If you undo an acknowledgement while the alert is still firing, the escalation continues with the next step it hadn't sent yet.

Changing an escalation's steps only affects alerts that fire afterwards. You can't delete an escalation while a monitor uses it.

On Appwrite Cloud, the Free plan includes 1 escalation of up to 2 steps, without repeats. Pro includes 10 escalations of up to 10 steps each. Every confirmed member that an escalation step names, directly or through a role, is an on-call responder. Pro includes 3 on-call responders, and each additional responder costs $25 per month. See [Pricing](/pricing) for other plans.

# Acknowledge with a server SDK {% #acknowledge-with-a-server-sdk %}

To acknowledge alerts from your own tools, such as a chat command or an on-call integration, create an API key with the `alerts.write` scope under **Connect** > **API Keys** in your project, and call `update`. Pass `acknowledged: false` to undo an acknowledgement. An alert acknowledged with an API key has an empty `acknowledgedBy`.

{% multicode %}
```server-nodejs
const sdk = require('node-appwrite');

const client = new sdk.Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')
    .setKey('<API_KEY>');

const alerts = new sdk.Alerts(client);

const alert = await alerts.update({
    alertId: '<ALERT_ID>',
    acknowledged: true
});
```

```server-python
from appwrite.client import Client
from appwrite.services.alerts import Alerts

client = Client()
client.set_endpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
client.set_project('<PROJECT_ID>')
client.set_key('<API_KEY>')

alerts = Alerts(client)

alert = alerts.update(
    alert_id = '<ALERT_ID>',
    acknowledged = True
)
```

```server-dart
import 'package:dart_appwrite/dart_appwrite.dart';

