```server-java
import io.appwrite.Client;
import io.appwrite.coroutines.CoroutineCallback;
import io.appwrite.exceptions.AppwriteException;
import io.appwrite.services.Alerts;

Client client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint("https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1")
    .setProject("<PROJECT_ID>")
    .setKey("<API_KEY>");

Alerts alerts = new Alerts(client);

try {
    alerts.update(
        "<ALERT_ID>", // alertId
        true, // acknowledged
        new CoroutineCallback<>((result, error) -> {
            if (error != null) {
                error.printStackTrace();
                return;
            }

            System.out.println(result);
        })
    );
} catch (AppwriteException e) {
    e.printStackTrace();
}
```

```server-rust
use appwrite::Client;
use appwrite::services::Alerts;

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let client = Client::new();
    client.set_endpoint("https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1");
    client.set_project("<PROJECT_ID>");
    client.set_key("<API_KEY>");

    let alerts = Alerts::new(&client);

    let alert = alerts.update(
        "<ALERT_ID>",
        true
    ).await?;

    println!("{:?}", alert);

    Ok(())
}
```

```http
PATCH /v1/alerts/<ALERT_ID> HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json
X-Appwrite-Project: <PROJECT_ID>
X-Appwrite-Key: <API_KEY>

{
  "acknowledged": true
}
```
{% /multicode %}

To read alerts, call `list` with [queries](/docs/products/databases/tablesdb/queries) or `get` with an alert ID. Both need the `alerts.read` scope. You can filter the list on `monitorId`, `name`, `status`, `severity`, `type`, `resourceType`, `resourceId`, `metric`, `escalationId`, `acknowledgedAt`, and `resolvedAt`, and use `Query.search('search', '<TERM>')` to search alert IDs, names, and metrics.

# Use project events {% #use-project-events %}

Every alert is also a project event that [webhooks](/docs/apis/webhooks) and [Functions](/docs/products/functions) can subscribe to. The event carries the alert as its payload. Alert events don't reach client SDKs over Realtime.

| Event | When it fires |
| --- | --- |
| `monitors.[monitorId].alerts.[alertId].create` | The monitor starts failing and opens an alert. |
| `monitors.[monitorId].alerts.[alertId].update` | The alert resolves, is acknowledged, or has its acknowledgement undone. |

Use wildcards such as `monitors.*.alerts.*.create` to subscribe to every monitor. Project events fire on every change, whatever the cooldown.

To tell the `update` cases apart, read the alert's fields:

| Field | Value |
| --- | --- |
| `status` | `firing` or `resolved`. |
| `acknowledgedAt` | When someone acknowledged the alert, or `null`. |
| `severity` | The monitor's severity when the alert fired. |
| `metric`, `comparator`, `threshold` | A usage monitor's condition. Empty for endpoint checks. |
| `value`, `peak` | A usage monitor's latest reading, and the reading furthest past the threshold. |
| `result` | Why an endpoint check failed, such as `timeout`, `status_mismatch`, `tls_expiring`, or `rule_matched`. |
| `latency` | An endpoint check's latest response time, in milliseconds. |
| `notifications` | `1` once Appwrite announced the alert to your team. `0` if the monitor notifies no one, or while the monitor's cooldown holds the announcement back. An escalation counts once, when it starts, even if its first step waits. Later steps and the resolve notification don't count. |

## Act on alerts with a function {% #act-on-alerts-with-a-function %}

On the **Monitors** and **Alerts** tabs, click **Deploy an alert function** to browse the **Monitoring** function templates, or **Send alerts to a webhook** to create a webhook already subscribed to both alert events.

| Template | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| **Alerts to chat** | Posts firing and resolved alerts to Slack, Discord, Microsoft Teams, or Google Chat, which it tells apart from the webhook URL. |
| **Pause on usage spike** | Turns off the function or site that a usage monitor watches when it fires, at or above the `MIN_SEVERITY` you set. It never turns the resource back on. |
| **Uptime incident log** | Records endpoint check alerts as incidents in a table you can show on a status page, and can notify a Messaging topic. Available on Appwrite Cloud. |
